{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.TraceSignFlipRegime","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TraceSignFlipRegime","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:QOU.Eightfold.HeckeBraidingNonSymmetric"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TraceSignFlipRegime.trace_sign_flip_iff_symmetric","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TraceSignFlipRegime.trace_sign_flip_iff_symmetric","kind":"theorem","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TraceSignFlipRegime.lean#L113-L137","doc":"**The trace tower is sign-flip blind exactly on the symmetric-braiding\nlocus.** With `tr 1 ≠ 0` — a Markov trace is normalised so this holds — flipping\na generator leaves the trace unchanged **iff** `q² = 1`, which by\n`double_braiding_eq_one_iff` is exactly `σ² = 1`, the symmetric braiding.\n\nSo at any `q` where the braiding is genuinely non-symmetric, and in particular at\nthe substrate, the trace tower is **not** sign-flip invariant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TraceSignFlipRegime.trace_inv_sub_trace","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TraceSignFlipRegime.trace_inv_sub_trace","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TraceSignFlipRegime.lean#L102-L111","doc":"**The sign-flip changes the trace by exactly `(q − q⁻¹) · tr 1`.** Stated for\nany `R`-linear functional, so it applies to the Markov trace `tr_M` without\nneeding any of its Markov properties — the whole effect comes from the Hecke\nrelation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TraceSignFlipRegime.inv_eq_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TraceSignFlipRegime.inv_eq_sub","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TraceSignFlipRegime.lean#L92-L100","doc":"**The Hecke relation forces the inverse.** If `σ` satisfies the Hecke\nrelation and `τ` is a right inverse, then `τ = σ − (q − q⁻¹)`. This is the skein\nrelation `σ − σ⁻¹ = q − q⁻¹` (AGENTS.md §7a) read as a definition of `σ⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}